    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d54">

                <pb facs="0159" n="119" xml:id="pg119"/>

                <head>54. Memorandum From the President’s Special Assistant (Rostow) to President Johnson<anchor n="1" xml:id="d54-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1967-02-10T14:50:00-05:00">February 10, 1967,
                            2:50 p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#flush">Mr. President:</p>
                <p>The meeting<anchor n="2" xml:id="d54-f2"/> might be broken into two parts: <list>
                        <item>—a decision on the message to London which is urgent;</item>
                        <item>—a discussion of the other issue posed in the attached
                                memorandum.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d54-f3"/></item>
                    </list></p>
                <p>With respect to the first part there are two questions: <list>
                        <item>—do we permit Wilson to go ahead with his formulation?</item>
                        <item>—do we extend Tet?<anchor n="4" xml:id="d54-f4"/></item>
                    </list></p>
                <p>I suggest, therefore, that you open the meeting by asking Secretary Rusk this question: Can we proceed down this
                    track while resuming operations at the end of Tet (6:00 P.M. our time
                        Saturday;<anchor n="5" xml:id="d54-f5"/> the last we can stop it is a
                    message dispatched 10:00 A.M. Saturday)?</p>
                <p>When that is settled, we can march through the other issues.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold"
                            >Walt</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d54-a1.xml" xpointer="d54-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d54-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d54-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Johnson Library, National Security File, Country
                    File, Vietnam, Sunflower Plus [2 of 2]. No classification marking.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d54-f2" n="2" xml:id="d54-f2-content">
                <p>The President, Rusk, and McNamara met in the Cabinet Room from 3:19 to
                    5:12 p.m. to work out the message that would be sent to Kosygin. (Ibid., President’s Daily Diary) No
                    notes of this meeting have been found. For the message, see <ref target="#d51"
                        >Document 51.</ref></p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d54-f3" n="3" xml:id="d54-f3-content">
                <p>A handwritten notation in parentheses by Rostow reads “on its way in 5 minutes.” The memorandum is
                    printed below.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d54-f4" n="4" xml:id="d54-f4-content">
                <p>The President wrote “No” after this sentence.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d54-f5" n="5" xml:id="d54-f5-content">
                <p>February 11.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
